brain training games like this one claim  to improve your mental skills we know  that if you practice a game after time  you'll become better at it but do they  make us better at performing other  cognitive tasks in our day-to-day lives  in 2010 a study published in Nature  concluded they don't the team recruited  thousands of healthy young and  middle-aged people to regularly practice  online tasks designed to improve various  brain functions like memory planning and  reasoning sure enough people got better  at the trained tasks but the benefits  didn't transfer to skills they hadn't  trained but the volunteers were all  under 60 and such spring chickens may  not have much to gain now a new study  suggests older adults could gain from  playing computerized games in California  Adam ghazali and his team have found  brain training can improve some of the  cognitive functions that become worse  with age like short-term memory and  attention they've designed a game called  neuro racer players drive along a road  whilst responding to signs that appear  on screen every few seconds this tests  how well they can do two things at once  Wow I'm dangerous here this is Adams  team recruited a group of older adults  aged between 60 and 85 none of whom  regularly play video games  they had their brain activity measured  then they took neuro eraser home and  played it for 12 hours over the course  of a month then they came back to the  lab the team attached electrodes to a  head cap to monitor their brain activity  while they played the game again if I  may ask what's your age I'm 63 but I  noticed that I forget sometimes near a  race the players were better at  multitasking during the game compared to  a control group and is good at playing  the game as people in their twenties  playing for the first time  they performed just as well six months  later the team also noticed more  activity in the prefrontal cortex the  region of the brain involved in  controlling decisions while the subject  was playing the game they'd also become  better at retaining information for  short periods of time and of sustaining  their attention these brain skills  usually decline as we age although the  benefits of brain training in younger  age groups remain unclear the team think  a more challenging version of neuro  Racer could do the trick but in the over  60s this racy little number may yet hold  the key to revving up declining mental  skills  you
 

